    "final_text": "I've written the CSV parser and serializer with no dependencies, and `npm test` passes all 58 tests. Nothing is committed yet; the new files are `src/csv.js`, `package.json` and four files under `test/`.\n\n**How it works**\n- `parse` and `createParser` share one parser that reads a character at a time. `parse` is just a single `write(text)` followed by `end()`, so the streaming results and error positions match `parse` by construction.\n- Between chunks the parser keeps only the current partial record and its position. It remembers a CR at the end of a chunk so that an LF at the start of the next one is treated as part of the same line break. A record is returned as soon as its CR or LF arrives.\n- On about 20 MB of CSV (300k records), `stringify`, `parse` and streaming in 64-character chunks each took about 120 ms.\n\n**Tests**\n- **Unit tests** cover each rule in your spec, including the exact line and column of every error type and which error wins when there are several.\n- **Streaming tests** split sample inputs at every possible two- and three-chunk point and one character at a time, and compare the results with `parse`. Another test streams a 200k-character quoted field in 3-character chunks; re-reading earlier input would make it far too slow to finish.\n- **Fuzz tests** use a second, simpler parser written only for the tests as a cross-check. They compare it with `parse` on 30k random inputs, compare random chunking with `parse`, and run 20k random round trips through `stringify` and back. I broke the parser on purpose twice to confirm these tests catch real mistakes, and they found both.\n\n**Decisions where the spec was ambiguous:**\n- **Byte order mark and columns:** columns are counted as if the leading BOM weren't there, so `'\\uFEFF\"a\"x'` reports column 4.\n- **Duplicate headers:** a duplicate is detected when its field ends, at the next delimiter, line break or end of input. So `a,a,\"b\"x` reports the duplicate, but in `a,\"a\"x` the stray `x` is reported first.\n- **Header with no rows:** `stringify([], { header: ['a','b'] })` returns `'a,b\\r\\n'` instead of `''`.\n- **An extra quoting rule in `stringify`:** if the output would start with U+FEFF, the first field is quoted. Otherwise `parse` would drop that character as a BOM and the round trip would fail.\n- **Empty-line records:** a record with a single `null` or `undefined` value is written as `\"\"`, the same as `['']`.\n- **Option handling:** `parse` ignores options it doesn't use, such as `eol`, so the same options object works for both functions. Its `header` and `skipEmptyLines` options only take effect when set to exactly `true`.",
